     I disagree.  Not that valkyries are strong, but that you
should stick with a strong melee character when first learning
the game.  There's more to nethack than simply fighting monsters.
If you got stuck in an obviously too small map without stairs
when running a tourist, you could read one of your starting
scrolls of magic mapping and see the level layout so know where
to search for secret doors and secret corridors.  Or with an
archeologist, you could dig your own corridors--or hole in the
floor--with your starting pick-axe.  Or a healer can apply
his/her stethoscope to walls and dead-end corridors to find
hidden things more reliably than with searching's random chance
of success.  Offhand I can't think of any other role's special
means of dealing with "hey, this map seems to be broken," but
there may be some.  I suppose wizards have a small chance of
starting with a wand of secret door detection or spellbook of
detect unseen, but I wouldn't count on that if I was at the
stage where I had trouble finding my way around.

     Sticking with valkyries and barbarians here, you're pretty
much stuck except for brute force searching (or kicking).  Once
you have a feel for how full levels look, you can narrow that
done by quite a bit and not have to spend seemingly endless
turns searching, but that takes playing experience.  And you'll
probably get that experience more quickly by varying the types
of character you play.


